				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/fieldstone" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1436137); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1436137&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607958862" alt="fieldstone" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1436137)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/fieldstone" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1436137); return false;" >fieldstone</a></span> <span class="odate time_1526604689 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 May 2018 00:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3803910">
									

<p>I think the key would be to focus on specific examples. This only covers generalities that could easily exist in real life, and as a result is no more interesting to read than real-life intellectual property law texts would be.</p>
<p>If you want to make something like this work, a better approach would be to mock up the application for a single patent. Invent some weird technology only the foundation would need, give examples of the problems they're trying to solve with it, examples of what went wrong with previous versions, maybe list some absurd tests they had to do to prove it always works. That way you'd be using the new format to tell a story. Very few of us would have any idea what a patent application looks like, so realism is way less important than packing it with interesting ideas.</p>

							</div>

								
					
